## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests RBAC in a realistic multi-tenant admin UI with progressive permission changes, ensuring isolation and correct UI cues.

## Key Concepts
- RBAC policies per-tenant
- Test data seeding and isolation
- UI visibility vs. API enforcement
- Role upgrade propagation without relogin

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright example sketch
test('RBAC per-tenant role upgrade', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAsTenantUser(page, 'TenantA', 'u1', 'viewer');
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
  await expect(page.locator('#user-management')).toBeHidden();
  await upgradeRole(page, 'TenantA', 'u1', 'editor');
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#user-management')).toBeVisible();
  await loginAsTenantUser(page, 'TenantB', 'u2', 'viewer');
  await expect(page.locator('#tenant-data')).toHaveText(/TenantA/).toBeHidden();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle flaky network affecting role-change propagation? 
- What assertions would you add to guard against stale caches after role changes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for feature flags coupled with onboarding flows ensure correct gating, deterministic environments, and resilience to network issues. It checks seeding, isolation, and cleanup in addition to UI correctness.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and API-driven toggles
- Test data seeding and environment isolation
- End-to-end UI assertions for multi-step flows
- Network fault simulation and idempotent retries
- Cleanup and restoration of test state

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright example sketch
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('new-onboarding flow gated by feature flag', async ({ request, page }) => {
  // enable flag for test user
  await request.post('/api/flags', { data: { userId: 'test-user', flag: 'new-onboarding', value: true } });
  // seed data and start session
  await request.post('/api/tests/seed', { data: { userId: 'test-user', env: 'e2e' } });
  await page.goto('/onboarding');
  // verify four steps render
  for (let i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    await expect(page.locator(`#step-${i}`)).toBeVisible();
    await page.click(`#step-${i}-next`);
  }
  // final dashboard
  await expect(page).toHaveURL('/dashboard');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you run this test across multiple browsers concurrently?
- How would you rollback the flag if tests fail in CI?

